{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 violates the problem's demand that proposals expand or strengthen DeepReason's grounded extension. A priority filter that removes every attack whose source priority is not strictly greater than its target can only delete attacks, never add them, so the set of accepted conjectures at the fixed point can only shrink relative to the unfiltered semantics. The target's own note that committed roots receive unique top priority and that no retained attack targets such a root does not add any new acceptance; it merely preserves an already-derived property. Because the proposal is a pure, monotone-deleting graph transformation, it does not expand the grounded extension and therefore fails the stated problem as written.","counterexample":["Let the base AF contain arguments {r, c, d} with attacks r->c and c->d, where r is a committed root and priorities assign r:2, c:1, d:0.","Base grounded extension (no filtering): {r, d} — c is attacked by r, so c is out; d is unattacked once c is out, so d is in.","Priority-filtered AF: retain r->c (2>1) and c->d (1>0), giving the same graph and the same grounded extension {r, d}.","Now alter priorities so c:3 (exogenous, unadjudicated) while r remains top-priority for roots: retain c->d (3>0); r->c is also retained since root priority is highest, so the extension is still {r, d}.","In no configuration does the filtered grounded extension add any accepted conjecture beyond the base {r, d}; at best it removes attacks, which can only remove accepted arguments (e.g., if r->c were the only attack on c and priority(r) <= priority(c), c would become unattacked only by deletion, and d could be lost if c->d is retained while c becomes in). Thus the proposal never expands the grounded extension and can contract it, contradicting the problem's 'expand and strengthen' requirement."],"target":"f92683ec613659ca149ff57810dc904dbfe4e595bd7ad364a4756664689e6db8"}]}